Assuming the ring oscillator’s supply current is dominated by charging the stage capacitances:

- For an \(N\)-stage ring,
  \[
  P \approx N\,C_{\text{eff}}\,V_{DD}^{2}\,f_{osc}
  \]
  so the average supply current is
  \[
  I_{DD}=\frac{P}{V_{DD}} \approx N\,C_{\text{eff}}\,V_{DD}\,f_{osc}.
  \]

- Hence the small-signal conductance seen at the supply node is
  \[
  g_x=\frac{d I_{DD}}{d V_{DD}}
     =N\,C_{\text{eff}}\!\left(f_{osc}+V_{DD}\frac{d f_{osc}}{dV_{DD}}\right).
  \]

So the small-signal resistance is

\[
\boxed{
R_x=\left(\frac{d I_{DD}}{d V_{DD}}\right)^{-1}
=\frac{1}{N\,C_{\text{eff}}\!\left(f_{osc}+V_{DD}\frac{d f_{osc}}{dV_{DD}}\right)}
}
\]

For the **3-stage** ring oscillator in the figure:

\[
\boxed{
R_x=\frac{1}{3\,C_{\text{eff}}\!\left(f_{osc}+V_{DD}\frac{d f_{osc}}{dV_{DD}}\right)}
}
\]

If you ignore the dependence of oscillation frequency on supply voltage, this reduces to

\[
\boxed{
R_x \approx \frac{1}{3\,C_{\text{eff}}\,f_{osc}}
}
\]

where \(C_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective switched capacitance per stage.

Assume an ideal tail-current source, \(V_{DD}\) and \(V_b\) are ac grounds, and transistor output resistances are neglected.

For a test voltage \(v_p\) at \(P\),
\[
v_x=-g_{m1}R_{D1}v_p.
\]

The single-ended transconductance of the \(M_2\)-\(M_3\) pair is
\[
g_{md}=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}.
\]
Thus, with \(R_F\) disconnected,
\[
\frac{v_{\text{out}}}{v_p}=-A_0,
\qquad
A_0=g_{m1}R_{D1}\,g_{md}R_{D2}.
\]

For matched \(M_2\) and \(M_3\), \(g_{m2}=g_{m3}=g_{mp}\), so
\[
A_0=\frac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{mp}R_{D1}R_{D2}.
\]

Including the finite output resistance \(R_{D2}\), the resistance looking into \(P\), excluding \(C_{\text{in}}\), is
\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}
=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}{1+A_0}
}
\]
or, for matched \(M_2,M_3\),
\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{mp}R_{D1}R_{D2}}
}.
\]

Usually \(R_F\gg R_{D2}\), giving the familiar approximation
\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}\simeq
\frac{R_F}
{1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{mp}R_{D1}R_{D2}}
}.
\]

Including the input capacitance, the impedance seen by \(I_{\text{in}}\) is
\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=R_{\text{in}}\parallel\frac{1}{sC_{\text{in}}}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+A_0+sC_{\text{in}}(R_F+R_{D2})}
}.
\]
